  "approved_angle": {
    "title": "Why Carmen wanted more time and another set of eyes on a venue campaign",
    "core_perspective": "We want enough evidence to make a useful recommendation. In office hours, Carmen considered how much traffic a venue campaign had received, looked at what visitors were doing after the click and recommended Jacqueline's feedback on the landing page. She kept the open questions open."
  },
  "sharpened_point": null,
  "strategic_purpose": {
    "pillar": "WARM",
    "content_type": "behind_the_scenes",
    "why_now": "Make the judgment available in guided support visible through a specific office-hours decision. This contribution to Watch Us Build an Ad Strategy shows how Carmen assessed what she could conclude and identified a useful next review. It builds trust without an offer pitch or another attribution story."
  },
  "audience": {
    "who": "Wedding venue owners who already invest in marketing and want thoughtful guidance on what to keep running, what to review and when to make a change.",
    "reason_to_care": "They want a useful answer to 'Should we change the campaign yet?' This scene shows how expert guidance can identify what deserves attention while being honest about what the available evidence supports. The campaign was for corporate events at a venue, not weddings."
  },
  "intended_shift": {
    "understand": "The amount of traffic and what visitors do after the click both inform what a campaign review can reasonably tell us.",
    "believe": "Expert guidance can offer a worthwhile next step without claiming to have established the cause.",
    "do": "When considering a campaign change, ask what the available evidence supports and which question needs a closer review."
  },
  "point_of_view": "we",
  "wia_perspective": "In office hours, Carmen was reviewing a venue's corporate-events campaign. She said it needed more breathing room before she could assess its success, and she was also considering what happened after visitors clicked. She described someone reaching the inquiry form and then moving away from the page. Her suggestion was to ask Jacqueline for feedback on the corporate landing page, while making clear that she hadn't reached a conclusion. That combination matters to us. A useful review should help an owner understand what there is to assess and what deserves attention next. We can recommend a closer look without turning an observation into an explanation. That's a concrete expression of our belief that good marketing decisions start with good data.",
  "supporting_example": {
    "scene": "During office hours, Carmen reviewed a venue campaign for corporate events. She questioned whether the available traffic was sufficient to assess success and considered both the visitors arriving through the ads and the landing-page experience. Later, she described a visitor reaching the inquiry form and moving away from the page.",
    "wia_move": "Carmen recommended submitting the venue's corporate landing page to Jacqueline for feedback. She also explicitly said she wasn't reaching a conclusion about why visitors weren't moving forward. Credit Carmen for the assessment and recommendation. Jacqueline was the suggested reviewer, not a participant whose feedback is documented here.",
    "client_contribution": "The venue's campaign and corporate landing page were the subject of the review. No client quote, explanation, response or subsequent action is supplied, so none should be added."
  },
  "narrative_outline": {
    "hooks": [
      {
        "style": "observation",
        "text": "In office hours, Carmen recommended a closer look at a venue's landing page while leaving the campaign's open questions unresolved."
      },
      {
        "style": "contrarian_belief",
        "text": "An expert review can be valuable before there's a clear verdict on the campaign."
      },
      {
        "style": "question",
        "text": "How do we decide whether a venue campaign needs more time or a closer review?"
      }
    ],
    "problems_desires_symptoms": [
      "An owner investing in paid advertising wants recommendations they can understand and act on.",
      "The practical question is whether the available evidence supports a campaign change yet.",
      "The owner wants to know what deserves attention without treating a possible explanation as an established cause."
    ],
    "main_point": "We match the recommendation to what we can reasonably assess. In this review, Carmen wanted more evidence and suggested landing-page feedback without declaring either the ads or the page responsible.",
    "transformation": "The owner gains a clearer way to evaluate guidance: what have we observed, what remains uncertain and what would help us make the next decision?",
    "cta": "No explicit ask. Close on the value of a specific next review, with the cause still open."
  },
  "format_plan": {
    "reels": [
      {
        "job": "Use one newly recorded brand-narrated Reel to make Carmen's reasoning visible. Keep the traffic question and the landing-page recommendation together rather than splitting this thin scene into multiple clips.",
        "cover_text": "When the answer is still open",
        "spoken_opening": "Carmen was reviewing a venue's corporate-events campaign in office hours when she considered whether it had enough breathing room to assess, and what deserved a closer look.",
        "beats": [
          "She said the campaign hadn't yet had enough breathing room for her to judge its success. That shaped how much she could reasonably conclude from the review.",
          "She also considered whether the visitors arriving through the ads or the landing-page experience might explain what she was seeing. Neither explanation was settled.",
          "While reviewing visitor behavior, she described someone reaching the inquiry form and then moving away from the page.",
          "Her suggestion was to submit the corporate landing page to Jacqueline for feedback. That gave the owner a specific area to examine.",
          "Carmen also said she hadn't reached a conclusion. We want our guidance to be clear about both the next question worth examining and what the evidence can tell us so far."
        ],
        "read_time_seconds": 70,
        "source": "new_script",
        "evidence_ids": [
          "ev_c021717b8b8e7d3ead3ed50d",
          "ev_537e72b4567eac710dcc48d9",
          "ev_004cb8119f029c4ce5740be0",
          "ev_14ef48cbd4aa7096d0164cf1",
          "ev_c72a21e1aadc0d3c02147548",
          "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "carousel": {
      "job": "Walk the reader through the assessment, observation and recommendation. Every slide advances the reasoning, with the corporate-events context explicit.",
      "slides": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "job": "Introduce the decision this review helps an owner understand.",
          "text": "What can a campaign review tell us before there's a settled answer?\n\nIn office hours, Carmen was assessing a venue's corporate-events campaign. She considered how much there was to evaluate and where another review could help.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_c021717b8b8e7d3ead3ed50d",
            "ev_c72a21e1aadc0d3c02147548",
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "job": "Show why the amount of evidence mattered.",
          "text": "Carmen said the campaign needed more breathing room before she could assess its success.\n\nShe was considering how much traffic it had received. Her assessment remained open because she wanted more to work with before judging the campaign.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_c021717b8b8e7d3ead3ed50d",
            "ev_537e72b4567eac710dcc48d9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "job": "Keep the possible explanations separate from a finding.",
          "text": "She considered both the visitors arriving through the ads and the landing-page experience.\n\nEither was a question to explore. She hadn't established that either explained the behavior she was reviewing, so the discussion stayed at the level the evidence supported.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_537e72b4567eac710dcc48d9",
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "job": "Supply the concrete observation without assigning a cause.",
          "text": "Carmen described a visitor reaching the inquiry form and then moving away from the page.\n\nThat was something specific to examine. It didn't tell her why the visitor moved away or establish that the page needed a particular change.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_004cb8119f029c4ce5740be0",
            "ev_14ef48cbd4aa7096d0164cf1",
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "job": "Make WIA's collaborative recommendation visible.",
          "text": "Her suggestion was to ask Jacqueline for landing-page feedback.\n\nCarmen recommended submitting the venue's corporate page for review. That identified a useful next source of guidance without treating a page change as the established solution.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_c78a9beda84e171c2eea9d56",
            "ev_c72a21e1aadc0d3c02147548",
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "job": "Show the significance of Carmen's explicit uncertainty.",
          "text": "Carmen also said she hadn't reached a conclusion.\n\nShe could name what she wanted to investigate while leaving the explanation open. We value that kind of clarity: an owner should be able to tell which parts of a recommendation are observations and which questions still need work.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 7,
          "job": "Translate the scene into a useful principle and end without an ask.",
          "text": "A useful recommendation should fit the evidence available.\n\nFor a venue owner weighing a campaign change, the next decision becomes clearer when we explain what we can assess and where we want a closer look. That's the judgment we want our support to bring, even while a question remains unanswered.",
          "evidence_ids": [
            "ev_c021717b8b8e7d3ead3ed50d",
            "ev_c72a21e1aadc0d3c02147548",
            "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "email": {
      "subject_options": [
        "What Carmen wanted to examine next",
        "A venue campaign review with an open question",
        "How we arrived at a landing-page recommendation"
      ],
      "preview": "Carmen considered the traffic, reviewed visitor behavior and suggested another perspective on the page.",
      "opening": "Hi {{contact.first_name}},\n\nCarmen's suggestion during an office-hours review was to ask Jacqueline for feedback on a venue's corporate-events landing page.\n\nThe reasoning behind that suggestion is worth sharing.",
      "argument": "Carmen said the campaign hadn't yet had enough breathing room for her to assess its success. She considered how much traffic it had received and whether what she was seeing might relate to the visitors arriving through the ads or the landing-page experience.\n\nWhile reviewing visitor behavior, she described someone reaching the inquiry form and then moving away from the page. That gave her something specific to examine, but it didn't establish why the visitor moved away.\n\nShe recommended submitting the corporate page to Jacqueline for feedback. She also said she hadn't reached a conclusion about why visitors weren't moving forward.\n\nWe want those distinctions to be clear in our guidance. An observation can help us decide where to look next without giving us enough evidence to explain the cause.",
      "transformation": "When you're deciding whether to adjust a venue campaign, it helps to understand what the recommendation rests on. What can we reasonably assess from the traffic available? What have we observed after the click? What would another review help us understand?\n\nThose questions make the reasoning behind the advice easier to follow.",
      "ending": "Good marketing decisions start with good data, including a clear understanding of what that data can tell us so far.\n\nDaniela & Whitney",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev_c021717b8b8e7d3ead3ed50d",
        "ev_537e72b4567eac710dcc48d9",
        "ev_004cb8119f029c4ce5740be0",
        "ev_14ef48cbd4aa7096d0164cf1",
        "ev_c72a21e1aadc0d3c02147548",
        "ev_99eeb361297e358ab40f614e"
      ]
    },
    "caption": {
      "hook": "Knowing what deserves attention next is useful guidance in itself.",
      "adds": "For a venue owner investing in paid advertising, a campaign review should help make the next decision easier to understand. That includes explaining how much we can reasonably conclude from what's available.\n\nIn office hours, Carmen was reviewing a venue campaign for corporate events. She said it needed more breathing room before she could assess its success, while also considering what happened after visitors clicked.\n\nShe described a visitor reaching the inquiry form and then moving away from the page. Her recommendation was to ask Jacqueline for feedback on the venue's corporate landing page.\n\nCarmen was also clear that she hadn't reached a conclusion about why visitors weren't moving forward. The page was an area to examine, not an established explanation.\n\nThat distinction matters when you're weighing a campaign change. We want you to understand the observation behind our recommendation and which questions remain open. A review can give us a worthwhile direction to explore while we're still gathering enough evidence to make a fuller assessment.",
      "ending": "Careful judgment means being clear about what we know and what we want to understand next. No explicit CTA."
    },
    "stories": null
  },
